  nonce: d67d  # d67d
  message: "STALENESS AUDIT 2026-09-21 (owner-requested). VERDICT: STALE -- THE WORK SHIPPED THREE DAYS AFTER FILING AND THE ROW WAS NEVER CLOSED. Closing `done`. This row has carried zero discussion entries since 2026-08-22 while the artifact it asks for has been in the tree since 2026-08-25.\n\nTHE ARTIFACT: docs/VERIFY-CLAIMS-SCOPE.md, created 2026-08-25T17:41:58-04:00 by \"docs(verify-claims): publish the caveat vocabulary and the four limits on a clean verdict\", extended since by WI-riros (2026-08-28), WI-zumoz and WI-lavut (2026-09-06) and WI-dosuh (2026-09-10).\n\nTHE SPEC NAMES THIS ROW. docs/hypergumbo-spec.md:123: \"Seven of the eight had no user-facing definition anywhere UNTIL WI-BOVOR; they are now defined for a reader, together with the exit-code contract and the limits below, in docs/VERIFY-CLAIMS-SCOPE.md.\" So the closure evidence is not my inference -- the published spec already credits this item by ID.\n\nTHE DELIVERABLES, CHECKED ONE BY ONE AGAINST THE FILED LIST:\n  (1) \"a confirmed verdict means no chain was found in code the analysis could adjudicate -- not that none exists\" -> the doc's section \"What a clean verdict does not mean\" (line 59), with five named sub-limits.\n  (2) \"the receiver shapes it cannot type today, naming them\" -> the caveat table, lines 52-53, defines `untyped_receiver` with the worked example `sock.sendall(payload)` where `sock` is an unannotated parameter, and `unknown_receiver_scope` as the whole-verdict closed-world statement with count, denominator and distinct method names.\n  (3) \"the built-in catalogue is deliberately STDLIB-SCOPED ... requests / httpx / urllib3 have zero rows\" -> section \"The catalogue is stdlib-scoped, on purpose\" (line 61) states it in those words. THE PREMISE IS STILL TRUE: python.yaml:575 records that third-party HTTP clients \"are no longer in the catalog ... the catalog is strictly stdlib I/O primitives\", and the eleven `requests`/`httpx` strings in that file are a short-name ambiguity exclusion list (lines 64-70) and header prose (lines 9-14), not rows.\n  (4) \"the exit-code contract for a consumer: 3 is fail-closed and must not be treated as success\" -> section \"The verdict ladder, and the exit code you must not treat as success\" (line 16).\n  (5) \"the untyped_receiver caveat kind should also reach docs/hypergumbo-spec.md\" -> spec:123 now enumerates the closed caveat vocabulary, `untyped_receiver` and `unknown_receiver_scope` included.\n\nONE DELIVERABLE WAS EXCEEDED, AND THE POINTER IN THIS ROW IS NOW WRONG. The filing asks the doc to \"point at docs/io-primitives-overlays/python-http-clients.yaml\". That path does not exist and should not be cited again. WI-riros went further than a pointer: the overlays SHIP IN THE WHEEL at packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/io_primitives_overlays/ (python-http-clients.yaml, go-web-frameworks.yaml), LOAD BY DEFAULT, and announce themselves on stderr -- and under ADR-0047 they make third-party egress visible without ever licensing a clean verdict, so a call they classify still counts as unexamined. `--no-default-overlays` opts out. That is strictly more than this row asked for.\n\nSCOPE HELD. The filing said \"documentation only. No code, no verdict change, no new vocabulary\", and the doc is documentation; the overlay shipping is WI-riros's separate change, not this one's.\n\nNOT ASSERTED: that every caveat kind is equally well explained, or that the doc is discoverable from README.md / SECURITY.md -- I checked that the definitions EXIST and that the spec links them, not that a reader arriving at the repository root is led to them."  # d67d
